# This file declares its own private type `cursor` and builds one. It never
# imports std/list. Another file of the same module does (b.kso), and from
# that moment `cursor` here resolves to std/list's pub `cursor`.
#
# Expected: `kanso run main.kso` prints `m/cursor 1 3`. The local declaration
# is the nearer one, and a.kso has no import at all.
# Actual: error[opacity]: `list/cursor` is foreign — only `list` builds a
# `cursor`; ask it for one through a pub function (module m), pointing at
# line 15 below. The same happens for a private fn named `step` (std/list
# exports a type `step`).
type cursor
  p

pub fn start n
  cursor n
